WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.777
Before moving to the assignment exercises, use this self-check to explain the ideas without running code.

00:00:06.777 --> 00:00:11.542
Why are explicit SELECT columns often better than selecting everything?

00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:16.072
Why does a missing value require IS NULL rather than equals NULL?

00:00:16.072 --> 00:00:22.822
Why should LIMIT usually be paired with ORDER BY when we care about top, latest, or oldest rows?

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:26.137
What should you run before a new UPDATE or DELETE?

00:00:26.137 --> 00:00:29.647
And what is the difference between COMMIT and ROLLBACK?

00:00:29.647 --> 00:00:34.977
Try answering each question in your own words before revealing the sample answers.

00:00:34.977 --> 00:00:40.042
If you can explain these ideas clearly, you are ready for the coding exercises.

00:00:40.042 --> 00:00:45.717
The goal is understanding the reasoning behind the SQL, not memorizing one exact query.

00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:49.757
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:49.757 --> 00:00:54.827
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:54.827 --> 00:00:58.387
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:58.387 --> 00:01:02.527
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:02.527 --> 00:01:09.587
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:13.757
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:13.757 --> 00:01:18.465
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
